    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **276 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented a daily owner-registration limit that rejects the creation of a new owner once 20 owners already share today's registration date, responding with HTTP 400.\n\nThe change follows the existing patterns in the codebase (the same shape as the `DuplicateOwnerException` handling), enforcing the rule at the service layer where registration dates are assigned:\n\n1. **`ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner`** (`service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`) \u2014 after the registration date is set, for a *new* owner it counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals the owner's registration date and throws `DailyOwnerRegistrationLimitExceededException` when that count is already \u2265 20. Added the `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY` constant and a `dailyRegistrationLimitReached(...)` helper.\n\n2. **`DailyOwnerRegistrationLimitExceededException`** (new, `service/`) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` mirroring `DuplicateOwnerException`.\n\n3. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** (`rest/advice/`) \u2014 a new `@ExceptionHandler` that maps the exception to a `400 Bad Request` `ProblemDetail`.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1Tests`** \u2014 new test `createOwnerRejectedWhenDailyLimitReached` posting 20 owners (all accepted) and asserting the 21st is rejected with 400.\n\nThe check is scoped to new owners (`owner.isNew()`), so updates to existing owners are unaffected, and seeded owners (which have a null registration date) don't count toward the daily total.",
